# Chroma content pack, The Five Callings: THE FAITH ROAD (White, the faith leader; moments 1 to 9 of the faith road in
# callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md; Pathways and packs thread, 2026-10-19, for v24, switched off until `callings` is in
# batch.PACKS). In the shape of ../art/art-rungs.lib (holder pairs), ../art/art-steps.lib (steps), ../art/art-events.lib
# (falls and wrongs), ../art/art-longshots.lib (long shots) and ../art/art-worlds.lib (world-only events). Syntax: header
# of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/SPEC.md, notes/library-spec.md and callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md (pack
# rules, calibration lessons, balance layout). Names in title:, grants:, takes:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from
# callings/catalogue.py, core/*.py and the base catalogue (chroma-library/earth_perks_titles.py); world lines follow the
# forms table in the brief (callings/worlds.py).
#
# The road: base [ordained religious minister] or [member of a monastic community], the Known rung [senior cleric], the
# summit [head of a faith order] (it refines [senior cleric] and [member of a monastic community]). Two holder pairs
# (W-A on the base titles, calls WG / UBR, cycle s1; W-B on [senior cleric], calls WB / URG, cycle s2): rate .5, gap 1-3,
# no share: line (the holds: gate does the drawing, as art-rungs.lib); no option there takes a new title, the next rung
# is an aims:, and grants: gives only a perk plainly earned. Two steps at real odds (the senior post at 30, the order's
# headship at 6; the five singles at the same chance, the pairs solved around them, no perk on failure), one fall and
# wrong (triads D1a), one long shot (triads D3b; titles at 3 with grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed) and one
# magic-only crossing (pairs D6a). Each life event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five one-colour
# options plus the combination half named in its header comment; a pair counts half to each of its colours and a triad
# a third, and chances are set so every colour's mean is the same inside each moment, so the file is even in every
# stage. per_year is the brief's, the yearly rate per eligible person (whoever holds a title in holds:, inside the gates
# of engine-conditions.py LIFE_CALLINGS and LIFE_CALLINGS_LONGSHOTS); drivers are the brief's, and on the fall this
# file's own reading. Every option of a life event leaves one or two footprints (the footprints brief): a title or perk
# it gives or takes, a mark the rules read, or a foot: just before chance:; lessons on at most 1 in 5 options of a
# moment, never twice the same words.
#
# Wrongs are pickable with plain labels, one closed act to each means colour, each marked and closed with a real
# backfire: a gift taken for a vote in a congregation quarrel (G, the old families' side), the old silver sold quietly
# for the roof (U), the old rite held without the elders' leave (R, defied an authority), a colleague's theft from the
# poor fund buried as the faith's leaders ask (W), the oracle's price paid in the magic world (B). The faith road's
# wrongs are about money, power and lies only: no wrong and no text here touches a child or a vulnerable person; the
# theft is of money, and no person is harmed. Every faith is treated with respect and no belief is mocked: no real
# faith, church, order, scripture, person, place, country, brand or year is named ("the faith", "the house of worship",
# "the order", "the faith's council", "the old words", "the rest day"). Nothing graphic; a death in these moments is an
# old head of an order's, plain and natural. The tribal world has no magic: the keepers of the rites and the spirits'
# fire are how the band tells its own faith. In the magic world the supernatural way is one choice among ordinary ones,
# never forced, always priced, and never the person's own gift: the oracle's word, on a closed: approval: line with its
# backfire. Modern Earth has no magic.
#
# Realm-ready (CONTRIBUTING.md): the road rests on the core, a commitment of faith with its rungs, years on each, and
# the act that takes the next one; only its Earth face is data. In a magic realm the same road stands as it is: the
# priest of a god's temple becomes a priest of a great temple, then high priest of the god's order, and an oracle may
# name them at a price; the realm's own orders come as titles on the same rungs.

# ================================================================ everyday pairs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W-A, the minister or the member of an order: calls WG / calls UBR (cycle s1)
# Both halves hold [ordained religious minister] or [member of a monastic community]: stages young_adult adult mature
# elder, age 23-95, rate .5, gap 1-3. Every colour's mean is 70 in each half (single plus its cross option, 140).

== the parish no one else will take
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per minister of religion or member of an order a year: about 1 in 6 are asked to take a hard post, a poor, far or shrinking congregation or a small house of the order in a hard place (estimate); about 4 lives in 1,000 are ministers of religion and 1 in 1,000 lives in a religious community (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-95
calls: WG
holds: ordained religious minister | member of a monastic community
tone: mixed
life: faith, work, community
horizon: years
roles: boss, elder, colleague
earth: the faith's leaders need someone for a poor, far congregation in a town that has lost its work, or for the order's last small house there
tribal: the keepers of the rites need someone to go to a small, far band whose rite-keeper has died and whose hunting has failed
magic: the temple needs a priest, or a monk or nun of the order, for the cold shrine of a mining town whose mine has closed
scene: {boss} rings from the faith's offices on a Monday. The congregation at the end of the valley line has had nobody for two years: a cold stone building, a boiler that leaks, fourteen faithful with an average age of seventy-eight, and a town whose factory closed ten years ago. The order's little house there is down to two. "Nobody else will take it," {boss} says, and waits.
scene W: Someone has to go, and the vows {N} took did not say only where it is warm. A post is a duty, not a prize.
scene U: {N} wants the facts before an answer: how many come, what the building costs to heat, what the town still has, and whether anything there can grow.
scene B: A hard post done well gets noticed. {N} also knows that two years in the valley could be two years nobody in the faith's offices remembers.
scene R: Fourteen people have kept a cold building going for two years with nobody to lead them. Something tightens in {N} at the thought of them, and {N} wants to be on the train.
scene G: {N} has seen towns like that one: the boarded pub, the closed shops, the old women who still polish the brass on a weekday. Places come back, slowly, if someone stays long enough.
scene tribal: Word comes up the river from the keepers of the rites: the small band in the far hills has lost its rite-keeper, the deer have gone from their valley, and nobody will go to them. The old keeper who brings the word looks at {N} and says nothing more.
scene magic: A letter comes under the temple's seal: the shrine in the mining town has stood cold since the mine closed and its old priest died. The roof leaks, the bell is cracked, and a handful of miners' widows still sweep the steps each morning. Nobody in the temple wants it.
- W | say yes, since the faith's leaders asked and someone has to go | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: say yes, since the keepers of the rites asked and someone has to go | magic: say yes, since the temple asked and someone has to go | chance: 65
- U | spend a week in the town first, and go with a plan for the building | door | aims: senior cleric | v: achievement, security | tribal: spend a moon with the far band first, and go with a plan for the hard winters | magic: spend a week in the mining town first, and go with a plan for the shrine | chance: 75
- B | take it, on the leaders' word that it counts toward the next senior post | aims: senior cleric | v: power, achievement | tribal: go, on the keepers' word that it will count when the many bands choose their next keeper | magic: take it, on the temple's word that it counts toward a great temple later | chance: 55
- R | go at once, and keep the doors open every night of the first winter | habit | self_control: + | body: light | v: stimulation, benevolence | tribal: go at once, and keep the far band's fire lit for the rites every night of the first winter | magic: go at once, and keep the shrine open and lit every night of the first winter | chance: 75
- G | take it, and stay for good: learn every one of the fourteen by name | habit | grants: a flock that follows you | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: go, and stay for good: learn every hearth of the far band by name | magic: take it, and stay for good: learn every widow on the steps by name | chance: 70
- W>U | take it for one year, as the rule allows, and report what the place truly needs | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: go for one winter, as the keepers allow, and bring back word of what the band truly needs | magic: take it for one year, as the temple's rule allows, and report what the shrine truly needs | chance: 75
- U>B | ask for a second minister as well, with a costed case the leaders cannot refuse | v: achievement, power | tribal: ask for a second keeper to go too, with reasons the elders cannot turn down | magic: ask the temple for a second priest, with a reckoning it cannot refuse | chance: 65
- B>R | say no, and stay where the work is already growing | mark: turned down a chance | v: self-direction, security | tribal: say no, and stay with the band where the rites already draw every hearth | magic: say no, and stay at the shrine where the work is already growing | chance: 85
- R>G | preach in the market square on the first day, so the whole town knows | grants: preaching | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: speak for the spirits at the far band's fire on the first night, so every hearth hears | magic: preach on the market steps on the first day, so the whole town knows | chance: 65
- G>W | ask the old faithful what they need, and keep the services they have always had | self_control: + | v: tradition, conformity | tribal: ask the old ones of the far band what they need, and keep the rites they have always kept | magic: ask the widows what they need, and keep the old offices they have always had | chance: 70
works: A year on there are more faces at the rites than there were, and the place has begun to feel alive again.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the faith's leaders find a way forward for the far congregation, and {N} sleeps well that night.
fails: The first winter is long and cold, and by spring there are fewer faces at the rites, not more.
fails: The faith's leaders are not pleased with how it went, and for a while {N} hears less from the offices.

== a quarrel splits the congregation
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per minister of religion or member of an order a year: about 1 in 8 lead a congregation or a house through a quarrel that splits it into sides, over the music, the words of the service or the sale of a building (estimate); surveys of congregations find that most go through a serious conflict every few years (estimate); about 4 lives in 1,000 are ministers of religion (catalogue share)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-95
calls: UBR
holds: ordained religious minister | member of a monastic community
tone: trouble
life: faith, community
horizon: months
roles: elder, colleague, boss
earth: two sides of the congregation quarrel over a change: the new music, the old words of the service, or selling the hall
tribal: two sides of the band quarrel over the rites: the new songs the young bring, or moving the sacred fire to the new camp
magic: two sides of the temple's faithful quarrel: the new hymns, the old tongue of the offices, or selling the temple's old hall to a guild
scene: The members' meeting runs two hours over. One side wants the hall at the back sold, and the money spent on a new roof and music the young will come for; the other side's grandparents built that hall with their own hands. {elder} speaks for the old families and {colleague} for the newcomers, and when they sit down both of them look at {N}.
scene W: There is a written constitution for exactly this: notice of a vote, a quorum, a fair count. {N} is tired of people who want to win more than they want it done right.
scene U: Both sides are arguing about the hall, but {N} suspects the quarrel is about something else: who belongs here now, and who decides.
scene B: Whichever side wins will run this congregation for twenty years. {N} can see who is going to win, and so, perhaps, can the faith's leaders.
scene R: {N} can feel the hurt in the room: the old man who laid the floor of that hall, the young mother who only wants somewhere for the toddlers' group. {N} wants to tell all of them to stop.
scene G: The hall has stood for sixty years, through quarrels like this one before. Halls are sold and built again; families stay, if they are not torn apart first.
scene tribal: At the fire the band splits. The young want to move the sacred fire to the new camp by the river, where the fish are, and sing the new songs there; the old ones say the fire has burned under the cliff since their grandmothers' grandmothers. {elder} speaks for the old and {colleague} for the young, and both look at {N}.
scene magic: The temple's chapter meeting runs late. A guild has offered good silver for the temple's old hall, and the young faithful want new hymns in the common tongue; the old families still sing the offices in the old tongue their forebears brought. {elder} and {colleague} both turn to {N}.
- W | call a members' meeting, and let the congregation vote by its own rules | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: call the whole band to the fire, and let every hearth speak in the old order | magic: call the chapter, and let the faithful vote by the temple's rule | chance: 65
- U | meet each side alone, and draft a middle way they can both live with | grants: settling disputes | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: sit with each side alone, and find a middle way both can live with | magic: hear each side alone, and draft a middle way both can live with | chance: 60
- B | back the side that will win, and make sure they remember who helped | aims: senior cleric | v: power | tribal: stand with the side that will win, and make sure they remember it | magic: back the side that will win, and make sure the chapter remembers it | chance: 70
- R | preach on it from the heart, and tell both sides they are wrong | self_control: - | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: speak for the spirits at the fire, and tell both sides they shame the dead | chance: 65
- G | take the old families' large gift for the roof, and speak for their side | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a gift for a vote; backfire: the other side finds out, and half the congregation leaves with them | self_control: - | v: tradition, security | tribal: take the old hearths' gift of furs, and speak for their side at the fire | magic: take the old families' purse for the roof, and speak for them in chapter | chance: 75
- W>U | set up a working group from both sides, with a remit and a date to report | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: choose two from each side to sit apart and bring an answer before the moon is full | magic: name a committee from both sides, with a charge and a date to answer by | chance: 75
- U>B | find out what the hall is really worth, and who stands to gain from a sale | v: achievement, power | tribal: find out what the new camp would truly give, and who would gain most from the move | magic: find out what the hall is really worth, and who in the chapter gains by the sale | chance: 80
- B>R | give the newcomers the evening service to run however they like | v: self-direction, power | tribal: give the young the night songs to sing however they like | magic: give the young faithful the evening office to sing however they like | chance: 70
- R>G | bring both sides to one long shared meal, and let them talk till late | door | grants: a flock that follows you | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: bring both sides to one great shared meal at the fire, and let them talk till dawn | magic: bring both sides to one long table in the hall, and let them talk till late | chance: 75
- G>W | keep the old service and the new one side by side, a week each | v: tradition, conformity | tribal: keep the old songs and the new ones side by side, a night each | magic: keep the old office and the new hymns side by side, a week each | chance: 65
works: By the next feast the two sides are sitting side by side again, and the question that split them is settled one way or the other.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the quarrel cools, and a year later the newcomers and the old families are running the summer fair together.
fails: The vote goes through with a margin of three, and a dozen families stop coming.
fails: The quarrel drags on for a season, and {N} is blamed by both sides for not ending it sooner.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- W-B, the senior cleric: calls WB / calls URG (cycle s2)
# Both halves hold [senior cleric]: stages adult mature elder, age 35-95, rate .5, gap 1-3. The next rung is an aims:
# ([head of a faith order]). Every colour's mean is 70 in each half.

== the old house of worship needs a roof
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per senior cleric a year: about 1 in 5 face a great repair on an old building the congregation cannot afford (estimate); old houses of worship need a major repair every few decades, and many close for want of the money (estimate); about 5 lives in 10,000 are senior clerics (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-95
calls: WB
holds: senior cleric
tone: trouble
life: faith, money, community
horizon: years
roles: boss, elder, colleague
earth: the great old house of worship is falling in, and the money to save it would feed the town's poor for years
tribal: the great lodge of the gathering place is falling in, and the hides and meat to raise it again would feed the hungry hearths all winter
magic: the roof of the great temple is failing, and the silver to mend it would feed the city's poor for years
scene: The surveyor's report runs to forty pages, and its last page is one long number: the roof of the old house of worship, four hundred years old, is failing, and a beam came down in the side aisle in the night. The same money would run the food bank in the basement for fifteen years. {boss} at the faith's council has written to ask what {N} proposes.
scene W: The building was given into the faith's keeping by people dead for centuries, and the poor are the faith's charge too. {N} believes there is a right order to do these things in, if only it can be found.
scene U: {N} reads the report twice and starts a spreadsheet: phases, grants, cheaper materials, what can wait and what cannot.
scene B: A great building saved is the kind of work the faith remembers, and the council too, when the order next chooses its head. {N} also knows which families in town have money and would like their name on something.
scene R: People are sleeping in doorways two streets away, and the council wants a fund for slates. {N} is angry before the meeting has even started.
scene G: The stones were cut from the hill above the town, and the yew in the yard is older than the roof. Buildings, like people, have their time, and {N} wonders whether this one's has come.
scene tribal: The great lodge at the gathering place, where every band has met for the rites since the oldest tellings, is sagging: its poles have rotted, and a beam fell at the last full moon. To raise it again would take the hides and meat of a whole season's hunting, and three hearths went hungry last winter.
scene magic: The great temple's roof is failing: a beam fell in the side aisle at the last feast, and the masons' guild names a sum in silver that would feed the city's poor for years. The temple's council has written to ask what {N} proposes.
- W | apply to the faith's council for a grant by its rules, and wait the turn | self_control: + | v: conformity, security | tribal: ask the keepers of all the bands for their share by the old way, and wait | magic: petition the temple's council for the repair by its rule, and wait the turn | chance: 70
- U | have the old silver valued, and sell it quietly to pay for the roof | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: the house's old silver sold without the council's leave; backfire: it is missed at the feast, and the faith's council asks where it went | self_control: - | v: achievement, security | tribal: trade the old sacred shells away quietly, for the hides the lodge needs | magic: have the temple's old silver weighed, and sell it quietly to pay the masons | chance: 75
- B | ask the richest family in town to pay, and carve their name over the door | aims: head of a faith order | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask the richest hearth for the hides, and cut their sign over the lodge door | magic: ask the richest merchant house to pay, and carve its arms over the door | chance: 65
- R | spend the money on the poor this winter, and let the roof wait | v: benevolence, self-direction | tribal: give the hides and meat to the hungry hearths this winter, and let the lodge wait | magic: spend the silver on the poor this winter, and let the roof wait | chance: 85
- G | close the great hall, and worship in the small side room that is still dry | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: let the great lodge fall, and hold the rites in the small lodge beside it | magic: close the great nave, and keep the offices in the side chapel that is still dry | chance: 70
- W>B | run a ten-year appeal with a committee, every gift accounted in public | grants: organising people | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: set every band a share to bring to each gathering for ten summers, and count it before all | magic: run a ten-year appeal with a council of givers, every coin accounted in public | chance: 70
- U>R | train a volunteer crew under one paid roofer, and let them do the rest | v: self-direction, achievement | tribal: have one old lodge-builder teach the young hunters, and raise the poles together | magic: hire one master mason to train the faithful, and let them do the rest | chance: 65
- B>G | rent the hall to the town on weekdays, and put the rent into the roof | self_control: + | v: achievement, security | tribal: lend the lodge to the traders between the rites, and take hides for the raising | magic: let the guilds hire the hall on weekdays, and put the rents into the roof | chance: 75
- R>W | climb up with the congregation every rest day, and mend what can be mended | self_control: + | body: heavy | grants: a flock that follows you | v: stimulation, tradition | tribal: climb up with the band each rest day, and lash new poles where the old ones failed | magic: climb up with the faithful each rest day, and mend what the masons cannot reach | chance: 55
- G>U | ask the oldest members what the building means to them, and put it in the appeal | aims: head of a faith order | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: ask the oldest of every band what the lodge has meant to them, and tell it at the gathering | magic: gather the oldest faithful's memories of the temple, and write them into the appeal | chance: 70
works: Two years on the roof is sound and the poor have not gone short, and nobody can quite say how both were managed.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the house of worship finds a way through, and the town is glad of it.
fails: The money runs out halfway, the scaffolding stands all winter, and the council's next letter is shorter.
fails: The quarrel over roof or bread divides the congregation, and {N} is left holding both halves of it.

== the young want the old rite back
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per senior cleric a year: about 1 in 6 find the young asking for an older form of the rite than the one their elders fought for (estimate); a turn back to old forms of worship among the young is reported across many faiths (estimate); about 5 lives in 10,000 are senior clerics (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-95
calls: URG
holds: senior cleric
tone: mixed
life: faith, community
horizon: months
roles: elder, colleague, mentor
earth: the young members want the ancient rite and its old words back, and the elders who fought for the modern service are hurt
tribal: the young of the bands want the oldest rite back, the long night of the masks, and the old ones who set it aside are hurt
magic: the young faithful want the old rite in the ancient tongue, and the elders who brought in the common tongue are hurt
scene: Eleven of the young members come to {Ns} study after the service with a printed booklet: the old rite in its ancient words, as it was before the elders changed it forty years ago. They want it once a month, at dawn, with the old chant. {elder}, who led the campaign for the modern service when young, hears of it the same evening and does not come the next week.
scene W: The old rite is part of the faith's own law, never done away with, only set aside. {N} would like the council's ruling before anything else.
scene U: {N} read the old words at the seminary and has half forgotten them. There is a great deal in them, and {N} wants to understand why the young have found them now.
scene B: Young members are rare, and these eleven are the congregation's future. Whoever gives them what they want will be remembered for it, in the faith's offices too.
scene R: {N} saw their faces when they chanted a line of it: lit up, the way faces look when they find something true. It is hard not to want that for them.
scene G: Rites change and come back like seasons, and {N} has seen it before. What matters is that {elder} and the young are still under the same roof in ten years.
scene tribal: The young of the bands come to {N} at the gathering: they want the long night of the masks back, the oldest rite, which the old ones set aside when they were young. {elder}, who ended it, walks away from the fire without a word.
scene magic: A dozen of the young faithful bring {N} a copy of the old rite in the ancient tongue, as it was sung before the temple's elders changed it forty years ago. {elder}, who led that change, stops coming to the evening office.
- W | ask the faith's council for a ruling, and keep to whatever it says | mark: kept your word | v: conformity, tradition | tribal: ask the keepers of all the bands to decide, and keep to it | magic: ask the temple's council for a ruling, and keep to whatever it says | chance: 80
- U | study the old words with the young ones, line by line, before deciding | habit | grants: scripture | v: self-direction, tradition | tribal: learn what each old mask means with the young ones, one by one, before deciding | magic: read the ancient tongue with the young faithful, line by line, before deciding | chance: 75
- B | give the young ones the rite, and be known as the cleric who brought them back | identity | aims: head of a faith order | v: power, achievement | tribal: give the young the long night, and be known as the keeper who brought them back | magic: give the young the old rite, and be known as the priest who brought them back | chance: 65
- R | hold the old rite with them at dawn, without the elders' leave | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: the old rite held against the elders' wishes; backfire: the elders write to the faith's council, and the cleric is called in to explain | v: stimulation, tradition | tribal: hold the long night of the masks with them, without the old ones' leave | magic: sing the old rite with them at dawn, without the elders' leave | chance: 70
- G | visit the elders who fought for the modern service, one by one, and listen | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: sit with each of the old ones who ended the long night, and listen | magic: visit each elder who brought in the common tongue, and listen | chance: 70
- W>B | lead the old rite exactly by the book, and invite the faith's leaders to see it | aims: head of a faith order | v: conformity, power | tribal: lead the long night exactly as the oldest tellings say, and ask the keepers of all the bands to come | magic: sing the old rite exactly by the rubric, and invite the temple's council to see it | chance: 60
- U>R | write a plain translation, so the young feel what the old words mean | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: tell the young plainly what each old mask means, so they feel it | magic: write a plain translation beside the ancient tongue, so the young feel what it says | chance: 65
- B>G | offer the elders the places of honour at the old rite, so the families hold together | v: security, tradition | tribal: give the old ones the first places at the long night, so the hearths hold together | magic: give the elders the seats of honour at the old rite, so the families hold together | chance: 75
- R>W | preach that both rites belong to the faith, and both sides to each other | grants: a flock that follows you | v: universalism, stimulation | tribal: speak for the spirits at the fire: both rites are the band's, and so are both sides | magic: preach that both rites belong to the faith, and both sides to the temple | chance: 70
- G>U | let the old rite come back slowly, at the great feasts, and see what it does | grants: leading a ceremony | v: tradition, self-direction | tribal: let the long night come back slowly, at the great gathering only, and see what it does | magic: let the old rite come back slowly, at the great feasts only, and see what it does | chance: 70
works: The old chant is heard again on the first morning of the month, and {elder} is there, at the back, by the second time.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the young stay and the elders stay, and the congregation is fuller than it was.
fails: The young drift off to a congregation across the city that gives them the old rite every week.
fails: {elder} writes a hurt letter to the faith's council, and the quarrel outlives the season.

# ================================================================ steps

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. the congregation needs a senior minister (pairs D1a)
# The door to the Known rung: every colour takes the senior post its own way at the real chance for it (30), and the
# post makes a [senior cleric]. White's way goes by the book, degree and all (`requires: a degree in theology`; without
# it only by the panel's leave, lacking .3). The pairs, all at 80, are the honest ways to weigh it: a smaller post
# nearby, a fellow minister's name put forward, the flock kept and the degree studied for, the congregation asked
# first, a year as the stand-in while the panel searches; none of them gives the title. Singles at 30 and pairs at 80,
# so every colour's mean is 55. per_year and drivers are the brief's; LIFE_CALLINGS gates it (six years on the base
# title, eight in an order, not a senior cleric yet).

== the congregation needs a senior minister
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-85
holds: ordained religious minister | member of a monastic community
tenure: 6-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .04 .05 .02
drivers: community+.3
times: per minister of religion or member of an order with six years or more behind them, a year: about 1 in 25 sees a great congregation or a large house of the order look for a senior minister and is asked to stand (estimate); about 1 minister in 8 comes to lead a great congregation or to oversee others (catalogue note); about 5 lives in 10,000 are senior clerics (catalogue share)
likelier: a congregation that has grown under them, a degree in theology, a mentor in the faith's offices, a town that wants someone it knows
rarer: a minister new to the faith's ways, a far posting nobody sees, a faith with few great congregations
gap: 3-8
domain: faith.5
tone: mixed
life: faith, work
horizon: months
roles: boss, mentor, colleague, rival, partner
earth: the great congregation in the town needs a senior minister, and the faith's panel will meet the candidates in the spring
tribal: the keepers of the rites for many bands need one among them to keep the rites at the great gathering, and they will hear each one at the spring fire
magic: a great temple of the city needs a priest to lead it, and the temple's council will hear the candidates at the spring feast
scene: The letter is on the faith's headed paper: the great congregation in the town, eight hundred members and a staff of six, needs a senior minister, and {Ns} name has been put forward. There will be an application, references, a trial sermon and a panel in the spring. {mentor} rings the same evening to say {N} should go for it; {rival}, it turns out, already has.
scene W: The faith's rules set the process down, and {N} means to keep every step of it: the degree, the references, the sermon, the panel.
scene U: Eight hundred members, six staff, an old building and a roll that falls a little every year. {N} starts thinking about what the place will need over ten years before thinking about the panel at all.
scene B: It is the post that leads to the faith's council, sooner or later. {N} runs through the names on the panel, and who knows whom.
scene R: {N} would rather preach than fill in forms. If the panel wants to know what kind of minister {N} is, let them come and hear one.
scene G: The congregation {N} serves now has known the family for years: the weddings, the funerals, the long winters. Leaving would be a loss on both sides, and the people there might want to say so.
scene tribal: At the spring fire the keepers of the rites for many bands will hear who should keep the rites at the great gathering. {mentor}, the old keeper, says {N} should speak; {rival} from the river band already means to.
scene magic: A letter comes under the seal of a great temple of the city: it needs a priest to lead it, and the temple's council will hear the candidates at the spring feast. {mentor} says {N} must go; {rival} has already sent a name.
