works: At midsummer the bell rings for the council's choice, and by evening the whole order knows what kind of years are coming.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the order comes through the choosing whole, and {N} is glad of the part played in it.
fails: The council chooses {rival} on the second vote, and {N} bows with the rest to the new head.
fails: The choosing drags on for a season, old friends take sides, and {N} comes out of it more tired than before.

# ================================================================ fall and wrong

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. a colleague's theft the faith would rather bury (triads D1a)
# A fellow cleric has been taking money given for the poor, and the faith's leaders want it handled quietly. Money
# only: no person is harmed, and nothing here touches a child or a vulnerable person. The wrong is White's single,
# White's own flaw: the leaders obeyed and the faith's good name kept, at the cost of the truth (hid a wrong; closed:
# approval, a theft buried; when the try fails the papers find it, and the flock's trust goes: takes_if_fails: a flock
# that follows you, as art-events.lib's forgery takes its name on failure). The others: the figures to the faith's
# council, the colleague told to repay and resign, the colleague faced that evening, the congregation told the truth,
# the police, a finance committee with two signatures, the question asked on the record, the fund made good from one's
# own savings, and the colleague helped to own up. Chances are set by act, and the singles solved around the triads
# until every colour's mean is 68. drivers are this file's own reading (hard times make a theft likelier).

== a colleague's theft the faith would rather bury
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
holds: senior cleric | ordained religious minister | head of a faith order | deacon or elder
per_year: 0 0 0 .01 .015 .01
drivers: harsh+.2 prosper-.1
times: per minister, senior cleric, head of an order or elder of a congregation a year: about 1 in 80 learns that a fellow cleric has been taking money given for the poor (estimate); audits of faith charities find money misused in a small share of them each year, and most cases come to light through someone inside (estimate); about 3 lives in 100 are elders of a congregation and about 4 in 1,000 ministers (catalogue shares)
likelier: loose books, one person counting the collection alone, a colleague in debt, a faith that prizes its good name above all
rarer: two signatures on every gift, an outside auditor, a small congregation where everyone counts the money together
gap: 8-20
domain: faith.5
tone: trouble
life: faith, work, money
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, friend
earth: a fellow cleric has been taking money given for the poor, and the faith's leaders want it handled quietly
tribal: a fellow keeper of the rites has been taking the furs and meat given at the rites for the hungry hearths, and the old keepers want it kept within the fire
magic: a fellow priest has been taking the alms given at the temple for the poor, and the temple's council wants it handled quietly
scene: The poor fund's book does not add up, and it has not added up for three years. {N} sits late in the office with the bank statements and the collection slips, and the gap has one name on it: {colleague}, who has counted the money alone every week since the old treasurer died. When {N} rings {boss} at the faith's offices, the answer comes after a long pause: deal with it quietly, for the sake of the faith's good name.
scene W: The faith's leaders have asked, and {N} has always done what they ask. But the money was given for the poor, and the faith's own law is plain about that.
scene U: {N} goes through it twice: every slip, every withdrawal, the dates against {Ns} own diary. The figures do not lie, and there is no innocent way to read them.
scene B: {colleague} has a great deal to lose, and {N} now knows exactly how much. That is a kind of power, and {N} turns it over carefully.
scene R: {N} has known {colleague} for twenty years, and the anger and the hurt come up together. There is no sleeping on this.
scene G: The money came from the old women in the front seats, a little every week for forty years. They are the ones {N} keeps thinking of.
scene tribal: The hungry hearths had less than was given for them all winter. {N} has watched where the furs and meat went after the rites, and the trail leads to the shelter of {colleague}. The old keepers, when {N} tells them, say it should be kept within the fire.
scene magic: The temple's alms chest does not match the almoner's book, and has not for three years. {N} follows the gap to {colleague}, who has counted the alms alone since the old almoner died, and the temple's council answers {Ns} letter with one line: deal with it quietly.
